course: dictionary
lesson: 10424
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word trolley is one of them. The sentence around it tells you which meaning is right. «The shopping trolley was full of apples and bread.» Here a trolley is a cart with wheels that you push.
explain: «The waiter wheeled the tea trolley to our table.» A waiter does not push a shopping cart, so this trolley must be a small table on wheels that carries food and drinks.
explain: When a word has more than one meaning, read the whole sentence and ask which meaning fits best. Then check it by putting your meaning back into the sentence.
fact: A trolley is a small cart with wheels for carrying things, such as a shopping trolley in a supermarket.
fact: A trolley can also be a small table on wheels for serving food and drinks, such as on a train or plane.
fact: In American English, a trolley is a tram that runs on rails in a street.
fact: «Off your trolley» is UK slang for silly or crazy.
fact: «Children never ride on trolleys, because they can tip over.» Stay close to an adult and keep your hands on the cart or the trolley.
exercise: «The shopping trolley was full of apples and bread.» What is a trolley in this sentence? | A bus that runs under the ground | A bowl for soup | *A small cart with wheels for carrying things | A pair of tiny wheels for a bike
exercise: «The waiter wheeled the tea trolley to our table.» What is a tea trolley? | A tall tower of books | *A small table on wheels for serving food and drinks | A bus that runs on rails | A cart for carrying children to school
exercise: «Children should never ride on a shopping trolley, because it can tip over.» Why should children not ride on a trolley? | Because it can tip over | Because trolleys are too quiet | *Because it can tip over and hurt them | Because trolleys are too pink
test: «In America, a trolley is a tram that runs on rails in the street.» What does trolley mean in this sentence? | A small cart in a shop | A table for serving tea | *A tram that runs on rails in a street | A wheelchair
test: Dad says, «You must be off your trolley to try that!» What does off your trolley mean? | Very tired and sleepy | Angry about the weather | Lost on the way home | *Silly or crazy
exam: «The more mass something has, the harder it is to push, so a full trolley is harder to push than an empty one.» Why is a full trolley harder to push? | It is painted red | *It has more mass, so it is harder to start and stop | It always goes downhill | It has fewer wheels than an empty one
